<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thondaiman dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1686–<a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Thondaiman dynasty ruled Pudukkottai in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> from the late 17th century until the state joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1948. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raghunatha-raya-thondaiman">Raghunatha Raya Thondaiman</a> founded the principality around 1686, and the family's support for the British in the Carnatic wars kept it independent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> and Ramnad. Pudukkottai was the only princely state of the Tamil country.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thondaiman-dynasty.jpg" alt="Thondaiman dynasty" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Linnaeus Tripe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Todiman_Raja_in_his_Durbar,_Pudukkottai,_1858.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1686 the Pudukkottai region was held by a chief called Pallavarayan under Raghunatha Kilavan Setupati, the Raja of Ramnad. Suspecting that the chief would shift his allegiance to the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a>, the Raja ousted him and gave the region to his brother-in-law, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raghunatha-raya-thondaiman">Raghunatha Raya Thondaiman</a>, the brother of his queen Kathayi Nachiar. The new ruler, a son of Avadai Raghunatha Thondaiman, had earlier ruled Thirumayam.</p><p>Though nominally feudatories of Ramnad, the Thondaiman rulers soon pursued an independent foreign policy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raghunatha-raya-thondaiman">Raghunatha Raya Thondaiman</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Tanjore</a> in support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai-nayak-dynasty">Nayaks of Madurai</a>, took the important place of Thirukkattupalli and, in a direct clash with Tanjore, conquered the land west of it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Later rulers helped the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcot">Arcot</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> and stayed loyal to the British. When Hyder Ali's army tried to invade Pudukkottai it was defeated and driven away, and the Thondaimans captured Kilanilai and Aranthangi; they also helped the British against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>. Menaced by a resurgent Mysore, which sought French support against the British, Pudukkottai came under formal British protection.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The dynasty kept a special Valari regiment. Its later long reigns included those of Ramachandra Thondaiman, from 13 July 1839 to 15 April 1886, and Marthanda Bhairava Thondaiman, from 1886 to 28 May 1928. The last ruler, Rajagopala Thondaiman, came to the throne on 28 October 1928 and ruled until 4 March 1948, so that the Pudukkottai Samasthanam remained under the dynasty for a year after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a>. He kept the title as a titular ruler until 16 January 1997.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thondaman dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Thondaimans</b> established an independent principality between Thanjavur and Ramnad.</blockquote><a href="/state/pudukkottai-state">Pudukkottai State</a></li></ul></section>
